<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ningbo</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mingzhou</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ningbo, known in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> period as Mingzhou, is a port city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhejiang">Zhejiang</a> province on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s east coast. Under the Song Dynasty it was one of the main ports, alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quanzhou">Quanzhou</a>, for maritime trade. Customs revenues from these ports were significant to the Song economy, and the city was a key link with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ningbo.jpg" alt="Ningbo" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nbfreeh · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tianyi_Square_2019-07_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ningbo is a sub-provincial city in the north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhejiang">Zhejiang</a>. Hangzhou Bay separates it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> to the north, Zhoushan and the East China Sea lie to the east, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaoxing">Shaoxing</a> and Taizhou border it on the west and south. Its administered area includes 531 islands and 1,562 kilometres of coastline. The name combines ning, meaning serene, with bo, meaning wave. Its abbreviation, Yong, comes from Yong Hill and the Yong River that flows through the city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest relics of human activity found in the city's area come from the Jingtou Mountain site in Yuyao, dating to 6300 BCE, and show early consumption of seafood and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>. Sites of the Hemudu culture have yielded cultivated rice, farming tools, remains of dry-fence buildings and domestic livestock. Ningbo was known as a trade city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> at least two thousand years ago, and the Ayuwang Temple near it is one of the nineteen Ashokan stupa sites in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><p>From the Tang dynasty Ningbo was an important commercial port. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> period, when it was called Mingzhou or Siming, Arab traders lived there in separate quarters, and a Jewish community was large enough that, after a flood destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torah">Torah</a> scrolls at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a> in 1642, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> of Ningbo sent a replacement. European writers long knew the city as Liampó. The Portuguese began trading there around 1522 and had a sizable community by 1542, probably on nearby islands such as Shuangyu. They raided Chinese ports and enslaved people, and were driven out in 1548.</p><p>Ningbo was one of the five treaty ports opened by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-nanjing">Treaty of Nanjing</a> in 1842. British forces took the walled city briefly in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-opium-war">First Opium War</a> and repulsed a Chinese counter-attack on 10 March 1842. In 1861 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiping-rebellion">Taiping</a> forces took the city almost unopposed and held it for six months. In 1940 Japanese aircraft dropped ceramic bombs filled with plague-bearing fleas on Ningbo, and the bacteriologist Huang Ketai reported at least 109 deaths from plague in November and December.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Ningbo had a population of 9.4 million at the 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. The port of Ningbo-Zhoushan is among the largest in the world by cargo. Its dialect belongs to Wu Chinese, and the city is known for tangyuan, stuffed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>-flour dumplings, and for seafood. The Hemudu site and museum, Moon Lake Park, excavated in 636, and the Ningbo Museum are among its sights. In 2016 the governments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> named it a City of Culture in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ningbo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Maritime trade through Guangzhou, Quanzhou and <b>Mingzhou</b> brought in large customs revenues, collected by maritime trade superintendencies, and connected Song China to Southeast Asia, South Asia, the Persian Gulf and East Africa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 2014 the Eastern Zhejiang Canal from Hangzhou to <b>Ningbo</b> was also recognised as an official component.</blockquote><a href="/work/grand-canal-of-china">Grand Canal of China · What It Is</a></li></ul></section>
